{"purpose":"Capture the intent of the 'Assigned Work' slice — the first team-collaboration requirement for the EKA ecosystem: assignee as data on work items (member unit + assigned-to relationship) and member-scoped view filters, without authentication or authorization, as the foundation for team work on projects instead of single operators.","content":"## 1. Background\n\nThe EKA ecosystem (eka-standard, eka-core, eka-cli, eka-mcp) models engineering knowledge for projects; today every project assumes a single operator. The board answers \"total work\" and \"per container\", but not \"what is mine\". Multi-member teams need lightweight ownership — assignment as data and member-scoped views — without building a permission system. Evidence and validated theme: fnd:team-collaboration (approved 2026-08-14), which locked the minimal slice 'Assigned Work' and the architecture constraint that the engine carries data only while authority (authN/authZ) stays in the application layer (ADR-019 D2 unchanged).\n\n## 2. Users and needs\n\n- As a member, I want `eka view board --member me` to show my items across all containers, so I know what I must work on.\n- As a member, I want items without assignee to stay visible in a separate \"No assignee\" section, so I can pick up work that has no owner.\n- As a lead, I want `eka view board --member \u003cmbr-id\u003e` to see ownership per member, so I can distribute work.\n- As a lead, I want `eka unassign \u003citem\u003e` to return a work item to the pool, so I can redistribute work.\n- As a member new to a repository, I want to create my member unit through the existing authoring path (`eka new eka/mbr:\u003cid\u003e`), so I can be assigned work without a new onboarding mechanism.\n- As an operator with an agent, I want work executed by my agent attributed to me, so my member view is not fragmented.\n\n## 3. Data model decisions (locked in planning discussion 2026-08-15, evidence cmt:team-collaboration-implementation-2)\n\n1. **Member unit `mbr-`** — new operating token family, home domain Execution (stratum 4), repository-level provenance (transits via the git snapshot like any unit, ADR-010/027). Workspace-level members are rejected: they never transit and would dangle at every repository gate.\n2. **`assigned-to` relationship** — new canonical relationship from a work item (sto/ts/bug/td/ch/spk) to a member line. Content attributes are rejected: projections derive from the model (relationships), never from content (ADR-013 contract); a content field would force schema changes across six work item types. Direction: recorded on the referring work item; member stays a passive target.\n3. **Single assignee** — at most one target per work item (precedent: replies-to exactly-one check). Multi-assignee is a future slice.\n4. **Conditional rule** — the new conformance check binds only units carrying `assigned-to`: R5 vocabulary gains the field (resolution + draft tolerance automatic), and R13 gains a conditional sub-check (target resolves, target is an mbr- line, at most one target). No new rule number; existing data without the field is untouched. Vocabulary, token, and sub-check ship in one engine version (an older engine rejects unknown relationship types and unknown tokens).\n5. **No by↔mbr conformance coupling** — `by`/`--by` stays free-string provenance (audit record, ADR-019 D2). Member-to-author display mapping is an application-layer concern (optional gitName/gitEmail content fields on mbr- are for display only, never validated).\n6. **Same-repo only, with enforcement** — assignment targets a member in the same repository. Enforcement point: `eka assign`/`reassign` verifies that the resolved target's provenance (project + repository) equals the work item's provenance and refuses deterministically otherwise; the R13 sub-check verifies the same at publish. Cross-repo assignment is a later slice (repository gates resolve within the analyzed set only; the store-wide resolver alone is not a guarantee — provenance must be checked explicitly).\n7. **New ADR-029** documents the token, relationship, conditional rule, advisory filter projections and non-goals (no authN/authZ); it is authored as the first work item of the execution container, deriving from this requirement. ADR-029 scope explicitly includes: the mbr- token registration (spec tokens/overview.md, tokens/mbr.md; engine token tables state.go/domain.go; reference-architecture token table where in force), the R10 exemption-list extension for mbr- (spec rule-011.md + conformance rules_domain.go — R10 today exempts only tkt-/ses-/draft knowledge artifacts, so the extension is a required change, not automatic), and the assigned-to vocabulary entry (relationships.md, rule-014.md R13 wording). ADR-008 needs no amendment (it does not enumerate the token table; new tokens declare their home domain per Core §14.2.7). ADR-019/001/013/017/018/010/011/027 unchanged.\n\n## 4. Scope IN (slice 'Assigned Work')\n\n1. mbr- unit: state vector (content + existence), home domain Execution, R10 exemption (via ADR-029 scope, see §3.7), required sections (purpose, content).\n2. assigned-to relationship: canonical vocabulary + R5 entry + conditional R13 sub-check (typed target, at-most-one, provenance equality, draft tolerance mirrored from R5).\n3. Commands: `eka assign \u003citem\u003e --to \u003cmbr\u003e`, `eka unassign \u003citem\u003e`, `eka reassign \u003citem\u003e --to \u003cmbr\u003e`. Semantics: assign sets the assignee — idempotent when the target is unchanged, refusal when the item is already assigned to a different member (use reassign to move); reassign replaces an existing assignment in one operation (refusal when the item has no assignee — use assign); unassign removes the edge and is a no-op exit 0 when there is none (precedence: relate unchanged). Commands are thin wrappers over the relationship edge with validation; assignment changes happen only through these explicit commands (no side-effect auto-assign from transition/note/relate/publish). Exit codes follow the transition pattern (0/1/2) with a machine-readable report.\n4. View: `eka view board --member me|\u003cmbr-id\u003e` — advisory filter (no auth); assignee tag on board cards in the unfiltered board too; separate visible \"No assignee (N)\" bucket below the six state columns in member-scoped views (rendered whenever non-empty, even in repositories with zero mbr- units); summary counts (Total/Active/Completed/Review Queue/No assignee) computed from the filtered projection; the container-axis \"Unassigned\" insight is suppressed or re-labeled in member views; --member composes with pagination (filter first, then window), byte-deterministic output preserved.\n5. Conformance and exchange: additive vocabulary, lossless round-trip (legacy items without the field stay valid), R13/R10 checks consistent, vocabulary + token + sub-check shipped in one engine version.\n6. Agent convention (see §7).\n\n## 5. Non-goals (locked)\n\nOut of scope for this slice: authentication/authorization/permissions (application layer, later phase); member registry CRUD beyond the unit itself; joint review/approval workflows; soft team isolation/visibility boundaries; sharing and discoverability; cross-project coordination; cross-repo assignment; multi-assignee; assignment history, notifications, mentions, deadlines; auto-assign policies; changes to container axis or execution projections; state domain extensions (assignee is data, not state); assigning through `eka relate` directly (assigned-to is exclusive to the assign/unassign/reassign commands). FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN hardening stays deferred (plan:eka-improvements outOfScope).\n\n## 6. Acceptance boundaries\n\n- A work item carries at most one assigned-to edge; the edge targets an mbr- line with equal provenance (same project + repository).\n- eka assign validates target type and provenance; unassign removes the edge (no-op exit 0 when absent); reassign replaces it (single operation, refusal without existing assignment).\n- `--member me` resolves the operator identity from git config user.name (the same authority as --by) to exactly one mbr- unit in the repository; zero match or multiple match is a deterministic refusal listing the available members (precedence: unknown --container usage error). \"me\" is a reserved keyword; a member id must not be named \"me\".\n- `--member \u003cmbr-id\u003e` with an unresolvable id is a deterministic refusal listing available members; cross-namespace qualified targets are rejected.\n- Default board output unchanged; filter composition deterministic (filter → group → page); exit codes follow view conventions; machine output keys pinned: assignee field renders the mbr identity, bucket key is \"no-assignee\" (never \"unassigned\" — that key belongs to the container axis).\n- Items without assignee are visible in member-scoped views in a separate \"No assignee\" bucket; never hidden, never interleaved with the member's own items.\n- Terminology: \"No assignee\" (member axis — no assigned-to edge) is never used for the container axis, and \"unassigned\" (container axis — no referencing ticket container) is never used for the member axis; `eka view board` help adds one line distinguishing both.\n- --member is an advisory display filter, not an access boundary: this slice has no authN/authZ; member views do not hide anything from anyone.\n- Legacy/imported items without the field pass validation unchanged; export/import round-trip lossless; absence of the edge means no assignee (no migration); done/canceled items retain their assignee as historical record.\n- Assignment change-log entries record `by` per the --by → git config user.name precedence (same as transition/note); an agent executing on behalf of an operator records the operator identity (see §7).\n- AI agents never appear as assignee values; agent-executed work is attributed to the operator via the existing change-log by mechanism.\n\n## 7. Agent-on-behalf convention\n\n1. Assignee is always a member identity (human operator). Agents never become assignee values — enforced mechanically because only resolvable mbr- lines can be targets.\n2. Agent-executed work is attributed through the existing change-log `by` mechanism (operator identity; --by override, precedence transition/note); member-scoped views treat agent work as the member's work.\n3. Agents do not set or clear assignment without explicit operator direction; no auto-assign; assignment changes happen only via explicit assign/unassign/reassign invocation.\n4. Work items created by an agent on behalf of an operator start unassigned until the operator assigns them.\n\n## 8. Evidence\n\n- fnd:team-collaboration (approved) — theme validation, minimal-slice lock, architecture constraints.\n- cmt:team-collaboration-implementation-2 — this planning discussion (published with this requirement).\n- alex-architect analysis (2026-08-15) and althea-product-specialist analysis (2026-08-15) — see review notes."}